ld/key "field-12" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab77a1c5-6aa2-5912-91c8-6b349f4b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419311} {:answer/id #uuid "b81d5084-d424-5514-bad7-8cfa5e02723e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b81d5084-d424-5514-bad7-8cfa5e02723e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419316} :answer-field/id #uuid "f50f4826-ab89-538f-b95b-5c4a1aa4f699" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ede85229-ac6b-539d-a1ef-b131f6c0e688" :question/math-academy-id "q-184797" :question/problem "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=$ {{field-8}} and $5b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}{{field-10}}{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}{{field-11}}{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}≡{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}≢{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca200dca-337f-5c83-b109-4c2f30d0acdc" :knowledge-point/title "Stating Counterexample Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ba3587-ad47-5715-bd71-005204af28b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3∣a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419283} {:answer/id #uuid "a4478efe-25df-5213-8187-f598d3ce28e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419305} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cdc3b5-e873-53de-8e6b-fd925dbe49a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∣a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ba3587-ad47-5715-bd71-005204af28b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3∣a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419283} :answer-field/id #uuid "612421c3-9ef7-51ac-9c53-676106d03320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9b9d97-f210-5e3f-9eac-9541bfee4624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∣a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419256} {:answer/id #uuid "387ccfaa-cabf-5956-8fb7-c24ffe7d7868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419261} {:answer/id #uuid "f34a320d-4941-51b7-bd57-825b87bc6011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "387ccfaa-cabf-5956-8fb7-c24ffe7d7868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419261} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad76e8a7-4f4a-5af2-bba2-21dbd8fbcccd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7920e3b-ba93-57c2-9dd2-0e80e521ff6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-221724" :question/problem "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $3∣a,$ then $6∣a$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,3∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,6∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(3∣a)\\,∧\\,(6∤a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n3∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,6∤a.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0394e4c4-97f7-556c-a7c1-ddf4cde28237" :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Divisibility Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b56fba-1fe8-5018-a849-c9135a952109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15∤6a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419244} {:answer/id #uuid "26df9e56-60bd-55d3-9930-e723ca75ed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419253} {:answer/id #uuid "92199c1a-c4ca-54d1-9352-05d4480730a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15∣a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26df9e56-60bd-55d3-9930-e723ca75ed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15∤a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419253} :answer-field/id #uuid "3936e0ea-927d-5098-a33d-5088fa2ab3ba" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c8ff3d-3b64-521f-b7d7-d365d141660b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :an