false." :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,15∣6a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(15∣6a)\\,∧\\,(15∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n15∣6a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=5$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=5.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15∣{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0394e4c4-97f7-556c-a7c1-ddf4cde28237" :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Divisibility Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dcda3d1-f189-5be2-a739-abf5eb27d43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a counterexample" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419265} {:answer/id #uuid "d75b58b6-7621-5439-b7f1-37bdedac30e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an example" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dcda3d1-f189-5be2-a739-abf5eb27d43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a counterexample" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419265} :answer-field/id #uuid "002d46cf-e110-5bd6-aff7-940eea1468e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :