id "2e74d768-0dbf-5b43-8d90-3d77b8d4a65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "≢" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419255} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e010d1-f63c-5f6c-b952-15b68d471658" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "513ba3a3-6200-5d9d-85ff-6dc4cf135103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419274} {:answer/id #uuid "7e319937-7d90-5a5f-b06b-6689d777b5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513ba3a3-6200-5d9d-85ff-6dc4cf135103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419274} :answer-field/id #uuid "feca1384-ecee-53ea-9957-2c042f82b064" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02cf0cdd-dd4e-5a78-a0c4-272aec9af99d" :question/math-academy-id "q-221751" :question/problem "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n0{{field-8}}2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,0{{field-9}}2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-10}} and $b=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n0≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,0≢2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203933 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca200dca-337f-5c83-b109-4c2f30d0acdc" :knowledge-point/title "Stating Counterexample Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344ee685-7c3d-57e1-8457-9b0689210e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3a-1<2b+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419259} {:answer/id #uuid "a5106851-b395-588c-905c-ad9005c512b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419307} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e722e4-ead7-50ff-9f49-951697330a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3a-1\\ge 2b+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0e722e4-ead7-50ff-9f49-951697330a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3a-1\\ge 2b+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419313} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d1962b-4142-509c-9371-2423b6456a30" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c68ef504-46c3-525c-a1b8-07db1530ac37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419323} {:answer/id #uuid "de7f6e29-7603-5a30-8a2a-41f42f815de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3a-1<2b+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419331} {:answer/id #uuid "eda59ec8-35cd-5dd6-af3f-e5afcd5000d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda59ec8-35cd-5dd6-af3f-e5afcd5000d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419337} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8ea50c-7960-5c8e-8b36-eeb9d466fff3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5678787f-ddbb-5c28-abd5-08d5ea9f1942" :question/math-academy-id "q-221959" :question/problem "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $a<b$ then $3a-1<2b+10$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,a<b$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,3a-1<2b+10$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(a<b)\\,∧\\,(3a-1\\ge 2b+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\na<b\\,\\text{and}\\,3a-1\\ge 2b+10.\n$$"}]}
